## Network Address Translation (NAT)

NAT is one of the most widely deployed techniques in networking. Understanding it is essential for
anyone working with firewalls, network design, or traffic analysis.

### Why NAT exists

IPv4 addresses are a finite resource. When the internet was designed, no one anticipated that
billions of devices would eventually need unique addresses. NAT was introduced as a practical
solution: it allows many devices on a private network to share a single public-facing IP address.

Without NAT, every device that wanted to communicate with the outside world would need its own
globally routable IP address. With NAT, the gateway acts as a translator — it rewrites the source
IP address on outgoing packets (replacing the private internal IP with its own public-facing IP),
and keeps track of those translations so it can rewrite the destination address on incoming reply
packets and deliver them back to the correct internal host.

NAT also provides a degree of implicit security: hosts on the internal network are not directly
reachable from the outside because they have no routable address. However, this should not be
confused with a proper firewall — NAT alone is not a substitute for firewall rules.

In this lab, NAT is used on the **gateway** to allow hosts on the internal LAN (10.0.1.0/24) to
communicate across segments. You are going to configure it from scratch.

### Setting up outbound NAT (MASQUERADE)

The most common form of NAT in small networks is **MASQUERADE** — a dynamic form of source NAT
where the gateway rewrites the source IP of outgoing packets to match whichever interface they
leave on. This is ideal for lab environments because it works without needing to know a fixed
public IP in advance.

==SSH into the gateway and add a MASQUERADE rule to the POSTROUTING chain of the NAT table:==
```bash
# On: desktop
ssh gateway

# On: gateway
sudo iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.1.0/24 -o ens19 -j MASQUERADE
```

This rule says: for any packet originating from the 10.0.1.0/24 subnet leaving via `ens19`,
rewrite the source IP to match the gateway's address on that interface.

==Verify the rule has been added:==
```bash
# On: gateway
sudo iptables -t nat -L POSTROUTING -v -n
```

You should see your new MASQUERADE rule listed with the source network and output interface.

### Observing NAT in action

With the rule in place, let's verify it is working and observe what it actually does to traffic.

==First, check the desktop can reach the webserver. Back on the desktop, unset any proxy
environment variables that might interfere with `curl`, then make an HTTP request:==
```bash
# On: desktop
unset http_proxy HTTP_PROXY
curl http://<%= $webserver_ip %>
```

If you get a response from the webserver, NAT is working — the gateway is translating your
desktop's source IP as packets cross into the DMZ.

==To see exactly what NAT is doing, SSH into the web_server and start a packet capture:==
```bash
# On: desktop (second terminal)
ssh <%= $webserver_ip %>

# On: web_server
sudo tcpdump -i any -n port 80
```

==Then, back on the desktop, make another HTTP request:==
```bash
# On: desktop
curl http://<%= $webserver_ip %>
```

Look at the source IP address in the tcpdump output on the web_server. You should see that the
incoming packets appear to originate from the **gateway's DMZ-facing IP** (<%= $gateway_dmz_ip %>),
not from your desktop's real address on the 10.0.1.0/24 subnet. The gateway has rewritten the
source before forwarding the packet into the DMZ.

> **Think about it:** From the web_server's perspective, all requests from internal LAN hosts look
> like they come from the gateway. What are the implications of this for logging and attribution?
> If the web_server were compromised and an attacker examined its access logs, what would they
> — and what would they *not* — be able to learn about the internal network?

### Making NAT persistent

The rule you added above is not persistent — it will be lost when the gateway reboots. To make it
survive reboots, save the current iptables rules:
```bash
# On: gateway
sudo iptables-save > /etc/iptables/rules.v4
```

This writes all current iptables rules (including your NAT and FORWARD rules from the previous
section) to a file that will be loaded automatically on boot by the `iptables-persistent` package.

> **Note:** Always save your rules after making changes you want to keep. If you need to start
> fresh, you can flush all rules with `sudo iptables -F` and `sudo iptables -t nat -F`, but be
> careful — this will remove your firewall rules too.